WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.994 --> 00:00:06.990
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:09.270
Hi, I'm John Helveston,
and thanks for tuning

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:12.180
in my lightning talk on how
to make R-powered surveys with

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.020
formr.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.578
If you're someone who has
ever had to make a survey,

00:00:15.578 --> 00:00:17.370
chances are, you've
probably used something

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.680
like SurveyMonkey
or Google Forms.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.220
And these platforms were
great for simple surveys.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.900
But what if you needed
to make something

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.880
that was a little
more complex than what

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.680
these platforms offer?

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.600
Well, thankfully, some
really clever people

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.600
made formr, which uses
R and R Markdown code

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.940
to create rich complex surveys.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.820
Now just to be clear, I'm not
one of those clever people.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:43.590
Formr was actually developed
by Ruben Arslan and Cyril Tata.

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:46.710
I'm just a formr super
fan and I made this talk

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.710
to share it with the
rest of the R community

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:50.460
and to demonstrate
some of the cool things

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.540
that you can do with it.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.390
So what can formr do?

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:58.170
Well, I kind of think of it like
SurveyMonkey plus R Markdown.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.360
You can use it to
create simple surveys

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.460
but you can also use
R code to generate

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.050
a much richer set of features.

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:08.070
For example, you could
create interactive charts

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.650
that survey respondents
could then experience

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.250
while taking the survey.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:17.180
Or you could use R code to
generate dynamic feedback.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:21.290
For example, images like this
were generated with ggplot code

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:24.530
for each respondent in a
longitudinal study showing them

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:27.500
their risk preferences for
different activities based

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.300
on their answers to
previous questions.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.800
So at this point, you might be
wondering how all this works.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.520
So let's dig into
some of the details.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.880
Surveys in formr are built
using open source components.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.950
The text and code
for each survey

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:42.900
are input using Google Sheets.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.060
And once the survey
is live, R code

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.460
is computed using OpenCPU.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.400
This makes it really easy to
make your work as transparent

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.260
as you like.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.360
So here's an example,
Google Sheet.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.360
Each row is an item
that will be displayed

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.560
on the survey and each column
controls what will be shown.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.490
The label column is where you
insert markdown or code chunks

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.570
that you want to
display in the survey.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:10.570
The type column is where you
set the type of each question

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:11.350
to show.

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:14.170
You can choose from a long list
of options like multiple choice

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:17.500
questions, text entry,
or simply just display

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.200
some text or images.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.740
Depending on the
type of question,

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:24.070
you may also have some
options for respondents

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:26.710
that you want them to choose
from like, in a multiple choice

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:27.820
question.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.530
And you can also
customize the look

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:31.870
and feel with some
styling options.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:34.360
It's analogous to an
R Markdown document,

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:38.620
where the style in question
type columns are like the yaml.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.440
And the label and choice
columns contain the content

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.050
that you want to display.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:45.830
Once you've got your
spreadsheet ready,

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:48.340
you can then log in
to formr.org, input

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.200
the link to your
Google Sheet, and formr

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.380
will convert it into a survey.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.660
The example I just
showed generates a survey

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:57.130
with some multiple
choice questions

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:01.370
about apples that look
like this in a web browser.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:04.460
Once your survey's live, you can
then use the associated formr

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:08.540
or package to go import results
in a nicely formatted data

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.550
frame directly into R.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.900
Now that we know a little
bit about how formr works,

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.750
I'd like to show a
couple of examples of how

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:18.210
I've used it in my own work.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.880
And I'll start with an
example of showing people

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.460
randomized images in a
research method known

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.840
as conjoint analysis.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:27.730
In conjoin surveys,
you ask people

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:31.000
to choose from randomized
profiles of different products.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.440
You can then use
that choice data

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.810
to estimate their
preferences for attributes

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:35.980
of those products.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:37.750
So for example, if
I wanted to know

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:40.420
how people felt about
the price or freshness

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:43.000
of different apples, I
could ask them to choose

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.620
from profiles like this.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.180
And then in each
choice question,

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:50.200
I'll randomly vary the
values that I'm showing them.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.290
Because I'm working an R, I can
predefine all those profiles

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:54.620
in a data frame.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:57.430
So in this example, the
three profiles shown

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:02.020
are generated from the first
three rows of this data frame.

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:04.720
I've also used formr to create
timed randomized quizzes

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:06.390
for my class.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:09.540
I do this by creating
a A and B version

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:11.550
of every question on a quiz.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:14.040
Then I use the sample
function to randomly select

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.720
a version for each question.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.703
In this example,
you might be asked,

00:04:17.703 --> 00:04:18.870
what's your favorite animal?

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:20.537
Or what's your favorite
color, depending

00:04:20.537 --> 00:04:23.040
on which question is selected.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.140
With just a five
question quiz, I

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.500
can generate 32 unique
combinations of quizzes.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.030
And in this particular
example, I've

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:32.730
also added a timer so that
the quiz will automatically

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:35.280
submit when the time is up.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.860
So that's just a few examples
of how I've used formr.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.700
But I encourage you
to check out formr.org

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.210
to see some of the
other complex designs

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:45.510
that you might want to make,
like longitudinal or diary

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:50.600
studies with automated email
and text messaging reminders.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.310
Today, over a
million people have

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:54.290
filled out a survey on formr.

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:56.900
And there's a growing user
base with a very active support

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:57.620
group.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:59.570
So you'll have all the
help you need to build

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:01.400
whatever survey you want.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.500
So head on over to
formr.org and start

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:06.130
making some R-powered surveys.

